  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM EST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nToday-Saturday... Surface high pressure spreads eastward across the \nregion as low pressure continues to lift across the northeast U.S. \nWest winds increase around 10 mph this afternoon, gusting 15-20 mph \nas the pressure gradient continues to gradually slacken. Light \nnortherly winds on Saturday become light and variable as the surface \nridge axis passes central Florida. Despite increasing upper level \nclouds, a deep dry layer between 850mb-500mb will limit any rain \nchances through the end of the week. A cooler airmass will keep \nafternoon temperatures below seasonal normals, with highs widely \nspreading the low 60s (north) to low 70s (south) each day. Morning \nlow temperatures mostly range the low to mid 50s, with mid to upper \n40s limited to far north interior areas of Lake and Volusia counties.\n\nSunday-Thursday... A mid level trough and its associated surface \nfront move through the southeast U.S. early next week. The front \nsags near the local area on Monday while ridging holds over the \nsouthwest Atlantic. Weak low pressure developing along the boundary \nlooks to more cleanly pull the front south of the area into mid \nweek, and high pressure building over the eastern U.S. begins to \nadvect a drier airmass Wednesday and Thursday. \n\nDry conditions persist Sunday with the next rain chances (20-40%) \nbeginning to spread the area from north to south Monday, and \nscattered showers (40-60%) persist within the forecast on Tuesday. \nMostly dry conditions then settle into Wednesday and Thursday, \nalthough developing onshore flow will a keep low chance (~10-20%) \nfor coastal showers each day. Temperatures ranging the upper 60s \n(far north) to mid 70s (south) Sunday and Monday begin a warming \ntrend on Tuesday, reaching the upper 70s and low 80s by Thursday.",
